<BaseLayout title="About | The Cosmic Notebook" description="Why Ojaas Hampiholi keeps a public notebook of clear, source-backed space-science notes.">
  <p class="eyebrow">About this notebook</p>
  <h1>Why I keep looking up</h1>
  <img class="about-visual" src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/images/midnight-desk.png`} alt="An open astronomy notebook beside a lamp and telescope under a night sky" />
  <p>The Cosmic Notebook began with a simple habit: finding something fascinating about the universe, opening far too many tabs to understand it, and eventually writing down what I had learned. </p>
  <p>It is a personal notebook built around a long-running curiosity about astronomy, astrophysics, cosmology, planetary science, space exploration, and the physics that helps connect them. Some days that means following an unexpected observation from a distant galaxy. On others, it might be a new space mission, an unusual exoplanet, a question about black holes, or a result that changes how scientists think about the early universe.</p>
  <p>The deeper I follow these subjects, the more interesting the questions seem to become.</p>
  <p>I am not a formally trained astronomer or astrophysicist. I am simply a curious and careful reader who enjoys following credible scientific research, understanding it well enough to explain it plainly, and keeping a record of the discoveries and ideas worth returning to.</p>
  <p>Wherever possible, every note links back to the scientific papers, observatories, universities, space agencies, researchers, or other primary sources that made it possible.</p>
  <h2>What lives here</h2>
  <p>The Cosmic Notebook is a growing collection of accessible notes on astronomy, astrophysics, cosmology, planetary science, space missions, space exploration, and related areas of physics.</p>
  <p>You might find notes about new astronomical discoveries, unusual objects in space, telescopes and observatories, planetary missions, exoplanets, stars, galaxies, black holes, gravitational waves, the early universe, or experiments that help us understand how nature works.</p>
  <p>The notebook is primarily written for general readers who are curious about space and science but may not have a formal background in either.</p>
  <p>My aim with this initiative is to make complex astronomy and astrophysics research easier to enter without stripping away the uncertainty, nuance, or scientific context that makes the original work interesting.</p>
  <p>Students, researchers, astronomers, physicists, and other experts are equally welcome here, particularly when they can point out something I have misunderstood or suggest a better way to explain it.</p>
  <h2>How I approach it</h2>
  <p>Each note begins with credible scientific or institutional sources. Whenever possible, I prefer original research papers, universities, observatories, scientific collaborations, space agencies such as NASA, ESA and ISRO, and other primary sources before turning to secondary reporting for additional context.</p>
  <p>I try to separate established findings from preliminary results, interesting hypotheses, and early signals that still require more evidence.</p>
  <p>Technical terminology is sometimes unavoidable in astronomy, cosmology, and physics. When it matters, I try to explain the idea rather than simply repeat the jargon.</p>
  <p>The goal is not to turn The Cosmic Notebook into an exhaustive feed of astronomy news or space news.</p>
  <p>There is already more science published every day than any one person could reasonably follow. Instead, this is a deliberately selective record of discoveries, missions, observations, experiments, and questions that seem worth pausing over, understanding a little better, and perhaps returning to later.</p>
  <h2>A note on scope</h2>
  <p>The Cosmic Notebook is not a scientific journal, research publication, or substitute for the original research.</p>
  <p>These notes should not be treated as professional scientific advice or as definitive interpretations of active areas of research. Science changes as new evidence appears, and some of the most interesting discoveries begin with uncertainty.</p>
  <p>Think of each page instead as a starting point and if something here catches your attention, follow the sources, read the original research, explore the work of the scientists involved, and continue the trail for yourself.</p>
  <p>That is ultimately what this notebook is meant to encourage: <i>Curiosity that does not end with the summary</i></p>
  <h2>Stay in touch</h2>
  <p>The Cosmic Notebook is also an open invitation to learn in public. If you spot an error, know of an important astronomy or space research source I should read, have a discovery worth adding to the notebook, or simply see a clearer way to explain something, I would genuinely like to hear from you.</p>
  <p>Write to my <a href="mailto:ojaas2013@gmail.com">Email</a>.</p>
  <p>Corrections, thoughtful disagreements, reading suggestions, and wonderfully obscure space rabbit holes are all welcome.</p>
